From bd27b063d46fc55457a798cb3e343dc01931c3a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adeeb Shihadeh <adeebshihadeh@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 19:58:44 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] qcom: support MSM DRM on the mainline kernel

When the DRM driver of /dev/dri/renderD128 is "msm" (the upstream kernel; the
downstream kernel's display driver there is "msm_drm" and its GPU is KGSL), use
the upstream GPU driver. Everything above the kernel interface is shared with
KGSL: the same command streams, signals and submission from the linked HCQ graph.

VM_BIND (Linux 6.17+) keeps the interface KGSL-shaped: tinygrad picks the GPU
addresses, maps BOs once, and submits an IB with no BO list. Each submit sets its
fence seqno to the timeline value the batch signals, so waits block in
MSM_WAIT_FENCE instead of spinning.

The submit ioctl is lowered into the graph, so a captured graph only works on the
kernel interface it was built for. Its fd and context variables are named after
the interface (kgsl_fd/kgsl_ctx, msm_fd/msm_ctx), so running it on the other one
fails at the first run with "unbound Variable" instead of issuing the wrong
ioctl. The submit's return value is kept, the first failure sticks, and waits
raise if a submit failed, rather than timing out on a signal that never comes.
MSM_WAIT_FENCE's ETIMEDOUT raises, and VM_BIND waits are bounded by the device
timeout.

QCOM_PRIORITY, KGSL's context priority (0 highest to 15, default 8), is scaled
onto the MSM submitqueue priorities (0 highest), so the default lands on MSM's
middle default and openpilot's 12 runs its models below the UI, as on KGSL.
